{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.ToLinearEquiv","lean:module:Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.Rank","lean:module:Mathlib.Algebra.BigOperators.Ring.Finset","lean:module:QOU.QBeta.TemperleyLiebTower","lean:module:QOU.Mathlib.TwoRowDimCatalan"],"declarations":25,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_restrict_live_det_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_restrict_live_det_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":451,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L451-L460","doc":"**Restricted to the live channels, the Gram is invertible.**\n\nThe positive half, and the reason the excision is a relocation rather than a\nloss: drop the dead blocks and the determinant is a product over the survivors,\nevery factor nonzero. This is `H_n/rad ≅ TL_n` in the diagonal picture — the\nquotient is not a repair, it is what is left when the zeros are removed. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det_ne_zero_iff_all_live","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det_ne_zero_iff_all_live","kind":"theorem","line":433,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L433-L448","doc":"**`G` is invertible exactly when every channel is live.**\n\nThe converse direction is the one the compute layer needs: wanting `G⁻¹` is\n*equivalent to* asserting there are no dead channels. Under convention D that\nassertion is false for every `n ≥ 3`, so any producer that inverts the full Gram\nis committing to something the convention denies — and now has a statement to be\nchecked against rather than a guard to fall through. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det_eq_zero_of_dead","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det_eq_zero_of_dead","kind":"theorem","line":415,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L415-L424","doc":"**One dead channel kills the whole determinant.** If any block is dead then\n`det G = 0`, however many live blocks sit beside it.\n\nThis is the sharp form of what convention D does: it is not that `G` becomes\nill-conditioned, it is that a single vanishing weight annihilates the product.\n`y_(1^3) = 0` at `n = 3` is enough on its own. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram_det","kind":"theorem","line":408,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L408-L413","doc":"**`det G = ∏ y_λ`.** The Gram determinant is the product of the block\nweights — immediately, since the matrix is diagonal. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.IsLive","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.IsLive","kind":"def","line":398,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L398-L406","doc":"**A channel is live when the trace can see it.** The live set is exactly the\nsupport of `y`, and \"dead\" is the complement.\n\nThis is the word the compute layer was missing. It is deliberately defined by\n`y_λ ≠ 0` rather than by `ℓ(λ) ≤ 2`: the row bound is a *consequence* of\nconvention D via Schur–Weyl (a two-dimensional fibre cannot antisymmetrise three\nways), not the definition of observability. Defining liveness by the weight keeps\nit correct if the convention ever moves again, and keeps the reason visible. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.wedderburnGram","kind":"def","line":389,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L389-L396","doc":"**The Wedderburn Gram matrix**: diagonal, carrying the block weights.\n\n`G_{λμ} = δ_{λμ} · y_λ`. Not an assumption — it is what\n`rem:gram-matrix-spectral-connection` derives from\n`τ(e_λ e_μ) = δ_{λμ} τ(e_λ)` for orthogonal idempotents. Stating it as a\ndefinition is what lets every statement below be a one-line corollary rather\nthan an argument. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_le_catalan","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_le_catalan","kind":"theorem","line":335,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L335-L363","doc":"**`rank G ≤ catalan n`** — the surviving dimension is at most `Catalan`.\n\nThe three hypotheses are the three things that have to be true for the\nTemperley–Lieb reading of the degeneracy, and the conclusion is *derived* from\nthem by rank–nullity, not assumed:\n\n* `hW` — `W` is a radical subspace (its combinations pair to zero against\n  everything). For the intended instance this is the image of\n  `ker(H_n(q) ↠ TL_n(q))`, radical because the radical is a two-sided ideal\n  containing the three-strand `q`-antisymmetriser.\n* `hcard` — the basis is the `T_w` basis, so `card ι = n!`.\n* `hdim` — the radical has the predicted dimension. Stated additively,\n  `dim W + catalan n = n!`, rather than as `dim W = n! − catalan n`: the\n  subtraction form would be a truncated `ℕ` subtraction and would silently hold\n  for the wrong reason if `catalan n` exceeded `n!`.\n\nWhat makes the conclusion worth stating is that `catalan n` here is\n`Σ_{ℓ(λ) ≤ 2} (dim V_λ)²` — see `corank_general` and\n`QOU.Mathlib.dimHLF_two_row_sq_sum` — so this reads: *what survives the\ndegenerate trace form is at most the two-row part*, for every `n`, with no\nper-`n` computation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_le_card_sub","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_le_card_sub","kind":"theorem","line":322,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L322-L332","doc":"**The rank bound, as a bound on the surviving dimension.**\n\nThe quotient `H_n / rad τ` is what carries the non-degenerate Frobenius pairing,\nand this says its dimension is at most `card ι - dim W`. Read with `W` the\nTemperley–Lieb ideal: what survives is `TL_n`, of dimension `Catalan n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_add_le_card","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_rank_add_le_card","kind":"theorem","line":291,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L291-L319","doc":"**A radical subspace of dimension `d` costs the Gram matrix `d` of its rank.**\n\n`rank G + dim W ≤ card ι` for any subspace `W` of coefficient vectors whose\ncombinations all lie in the radical of the pairing. Equivalently\n`corank G ≥ dim W`.\n\nThis is the general form of the convention-D degeneracy. Instantiated with `W`\nthe image of the Temperley–Lieb ideal `ker(H_n(q) ↠ TL_n(q))` — which is a\nradical subspace precisely because the radical is a two-sided ideal\n(`TemperleyLiebTower.traceRadical_two_sided`) containing the three-strand\n`q`-antisymmetriser — it gives `rank G ≤ Catalan n`, the upper half of the\ncorank determination. Nothing here is special to convention D, to `H_n(q)`, or\nto any particular `q`: it is rank–nullity, and it is why the degeneracy cannot\nbe a precision artefact. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.mem_ker_mulVecLin_of_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.mem_ker_mulVecLin_of_radical","kind":"theorem","line":277,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L277-L288","doc":"**A radical combination is a kernel vector of `G`.**\n\nThe algebra-side condition (`Σ c_j b_j` pairs to zero against every `b_i`) and\nthe matrix-side condition (`c ∈ ker (G *ᵥ ·)`) are the same statement read\nthrough `gram_mulVec`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_eight_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_eight_value","kind":"theorem","line":241,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L241-L243","doc":"**`n = 8`: `38890`.** Prediction, as for `n = 7`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_seven_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_seven_value","kind":"theorem","line":235,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L235-L239","doc":"**`n = 7`: `4611`.** An arithmetic *prediction* — the number a Gram-rank\nmeasurement at `n = 7` would have to produce. No such measurement exists; the\nprobe reaches `n = 6`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_six_value","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_six_value","kind":"theorem","line":229,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L229-L233","doc":"**`n = 6`: corank `588`** — the value\n`computations/probes/tl_n6_discriminator.py` measured, now an instance of\n`corank_general` rather than a separate arithmetic check. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_general","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_general","kind":"theorem","line":217,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L217-L227","doc":"**The corank accounting for general `n`**: `n!` minus the two-row\nsquare-sum is `n! − catalan n`.\n\nSupersedes `corank_three` / `_four` / `_five`, which are the `n = 3, 4, 5`\ninstances with their block dimensions written out by hand. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_five","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_five","kind":"theorem","line":192,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L192-L195","doc":"**`n = 5`: `dim H₅ = 120`, `dim TL₅ = 42`, corank `78`.** The three-row and\nlower blocks of `S₅` — dimensions `6, 5, 4, 1` — give `6² + 5² + 4² + 1² = 78`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_four","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_four","kind":"theorem","line":186,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L186-L190","doc":"**`n = 4`: `dim H₄ = 24`, `dim TL₄ = 14`, so `G₄` has corank `10`** — the\n`(2,1,1)` block of dimension `3` and the `(1⁴)` block of dimension `1`,\ncontributing `3² + 1² = 10`. Same statement as\n`TemperleyLiebTower.radical_four`, written as a corank. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_three","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.corank_three","kind":"theorem","line":182,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L182-L184","doc":"**`n = 3`: `dim H₃ = 6`, `dim TL₃ = 5`, so `G₃` has corank `1`.** The single\nlost direction is the antisymmetriser — the one-dimensional `(1³)` block. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.no_radical_combination_of_det_ne_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.no_radical_combination_of_det_ne_zero","kind":"theorem","line":157,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L157-L169","doc":"**Contrapositive, the form the content blocks should cite.**\n\n`det G ≠ 0` *implies* no basis combination lies in the radical. So a block that\nwants `G⁻¹` is committing to a trace with trivial radical — which convention D\nis not. Stating it this way makes the dependency visible at the point of use\ninstead of buried in a normalisation. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_det_eq_zero_of_radical_combination","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_det_eq_zero_of_radical_combination","kind":"theorem","line":132,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L132-L155","doc":"**The Markov–Gram determinant vanishes when the radical meets the span.**\n\nIf some nonzero coefficient vector `c` produces a combination `a = Σ c_j b_j`\nlying in the trace radical, then `c` is a nonzero kernel vector of `G`, so\n`det G = 0`.\n\nThis is the theorem that refutes the eleven `gram-inverse-unqualified` blocks.\nUnder convention **D** its hypothesis is *met*, by `block_mem_radical` applied to\nthe antisymmetric block that `conventionD_kills_antisymmetriser` sends to zero —\nso `G⁻¹` does not exist and the co-Casimir `Σ (G⁻¹)_{ij} b_i b_j` is not a\nwell-formed expression on `H_n`.\n\nNote the hypothesis is about `τ(b_i · a)`, the radical element on the **right**;\n`traceRadical` is stated with it on the left, and `traceRadical_star_eq` /\ncyclicity is what connects the two. It is taken as a hypothesis here rather than\nderived so the theorem holds for a bare linear functional, cyclic or not. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_mulVec","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_mulVec","kind":"theorem","line":122,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L122-L130","doc":"**`G` applied to a coefficient vector is the pairing against the combination.**\n\n`(G *ᵥ c)_i = τ(b_i · Σ_j c_j b_j)`. The bridge between the matrix picture the\ncontent blocks use and the algebra picture the radical lives in. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_apply","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram_apply","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L117-L118","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.gram","kind":"def","line":111,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L111-L114","doc":"**The Markov–Gram matrix** `G_{ij} = τ(b_i · b_j)` of a linear functional\nagainst a finite family. No basis hypothesis: the family may be dependent, and\nthe statements below are about the matrix regardless. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.mem_radical_of_pairing_zero","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.mem_radical_of_pairing_zero","kind":"theorem","line":91,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L91-L101","doc":"**Non-degeneracy after the quotient.**\n\nAn element pairing to zero against everything *is* in the radical, so its class\nin `A / rad τ` is zero. This is definitional — `traceRadical` is defined by that\nvery condition — and it is stated anyway because it is the half of the descent\nthe content blocks actually cite: \"the induced pairing on the quotient is\nnon-degenerate\" is not an extra theorem to prove, it is what quotienting by the\nradical *means*. Saying so explicitly is what stops the claim drifting back onto\n`H_n`. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.pairing_descends","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.pairing_descends","kind":"theorem","line":78,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L78-L89","doc":"**The pairing is constant on radical cosets**, hence descends to `A / rad τ`.\n\nWith `traceRadical_two_sided` (the radical is a two-sided ideal, so the quotient\nis a ring) this is the statement that the Frobenius pairing the content blocks\nwant is well defined — on the quotient. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.block_mem_radical","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.QBeta.MarkovGramDegenerate.block_mem_radical","kind":"theorem","line":60,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/QBeta/MarkovGramDegenerate.lean#L60-L76","doc":"**A zero-weight Wedderburn block lies in the trace radical.**\n\nIf the trace restricted to the block `p` is `τ(x·p) = y·χ(x)` and the weight `y`\nvanishes, then `p` pairs to zero against everything, which is exactly membership\nin `traceRadical`.\n\nCyclicity is the whole content: `traceRadical` is stated with the radical\nelement on the **left** (`τ(p·b) = 0`), while the block hypothesis is stated\nwith it on the **right**. For the Markov trace cyclicity is the defining\nproperty, so this costs nothing — but it is a real hypothesis and the proof\nwould not go through without it. ","signatureChunk":0}]}